{
 "id": "lru-cache",
 "title": "LRU Cache",
 "pattern": "hashing",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>A feed service keeps the profiles it served most recently in a small in-memory cache, so a burst of views on one creator does not hit the database every time. When the cache is full, it drops the profile that was used <b>least recently</b>.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(capacity, ops)</code>. The cache holds at most <code>capacity</code> keys. <code>ops</code> is a list of operations, run in order:</p><ul><li><code>[\"put\", key, value]</code> stores <code>value</code> under <code>key</code>. If the key exists, update its value. If it is new and the cache is full, first remove the least recently used key.</li><li><code>[\"get\", key]</code> reads the value, or <code>-1</code> if the key is not cached.</li></ul><p>A successful <code>get</code> and every <code>put</code> count as a use and make that key the most recent. Return a list with the result of every <code>get</code>, in order (puts add nothing).</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "capacity = 2, ops = [[\"put\", 1, 1], [\"put\", 2, 2], [\"get\", 1], [\"put\", 3, 3], [\"get\", 2], [\"put\", 4, 4], [\"get\", 1], [\"get\", 3], [\"get\", 4]]",
   "output": "[1, -1, -1, 3, 4]",
   "why": "The LeetCode 146 example. <code>get 1</code> makes 1 recent, so <code>put 3</code> evicts 2. Then <code>put 4</code> evicts 1 (3 was used after it)."
  },
  {
   "input": "capacity = 1, ops = [[\"put\", 7, 70], [\"get\", 7], [\"put\", 8, 80], [\"get\", 7], [\"get\", 8]]",
   "output": "[70, -1, 80]",
   "why": "With room for one key, every new key evicts the old one."
  },
  {
   "input": "capacity = 2, ops = [[\"put\", 1, 10], [\"put\", 2, 20], [\"put\", 1, 15], [\"put\", 3, 30], [\"get\", 1], [\"get\", 2], [\"get\", 3]]",
   "output": "[15, -1, 30]",
   "why": "Putting key 1 again updates it to 15 and makes it recent, so key 2 is the one evicted."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "1 &le; capacity &le; 3,000",
  "0 &le; len(ops) &le; 200,000",
  "keys are integers, -10^9 &le; key &le; 10^9",
  "0 &le; value &le; 10^9, so <code>-1</code> always means \"missing\""
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(capacity, ops):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What does each operation need to be fast at?",
   "a": "Two jobs: find a key's value (a lookup), and know which key was used longest ago (an order). Both <code>get</code> and <code>put</code> touch the order, so both jobs must be O(1) for the cache to be worth it."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Capacity 2: put 1, put 2, get 1, put 3. What is evicted?",
   "a": "Order after the puts (oldest first): <code>[1, 2]</code>. <code>get 1</code> moves 1 to the end: <code>[2, 1]</code>. <code>put 3</code> is new and the cache is full, so drop the front, 2: <code>[1, 3]</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the simplest thing that works?",
   "a": "A dict for values plus a Python list of keys in use order. On every use, <code>list.remove(key)</code> and <code>append(key)</code>; evict with <code>pop(0)</code>. Correct, but remove and pop(0) shift the list: O(capacity) per operation."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Which pattern?",
   "a": "<b>Hashing plus an ordered structure</b>. A hash map gives O(1) lookup; a doubly linked list gives O(1) move-to-end and remove-oldest. Python's <code>OrderedDict</code> is exactly that pair: <code>move_to_end(key)</code> and <code>popitem(last=False)</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Make an empty <code>OrderedDict</code> and a result list.</li><li><code>get</code>: if missing, append -1. Otherwise <code>move_to_end</code> and append the value.</li><li><code>put</code>: if present, update and <code>move_to_end</code>. If new: when full, <code>popitem(last=False)</code>; then insert (new keys go at the end).</li><li>Return the results.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "Putting an existing key when full (must not evict). A <code>get</code> of a missing key (must not change the order). Capacity 1. Value 0 (falsy, but present). No gets at all (return <code>[]</code>)."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "You need two things in O(1): look up a key, and find the least recently used key. One structure alone does not give you both, so pair a dict with something that keeps order.",
  "<code>from collections import OrderedDict</code>. <code>cache.move_to_end(key)</code> marks a key as most recent; <code>cache.popitem(last=False)</code> removes the oldest.",
  "Only evict when the key is <b>new</b> and <code>len(cache) == capacity</code>. Updating an existing key never evicts anything."
 ],
 "solution": "from collections import OrderedDict\n\ndef solution(capacity, ops):\n    cache = OrderedDict()          # oldest key first, most recent last\n    results = []\n    for op in ops:\n        if op[0] == \"get\":\n            key = op[1]\n            if key not in cache:\n                results.append(-1)\n                continue\n            cache.move_to_end(key)     # a hit makes the key the most recent\n            results.append(cache[key])\n        else:\n            key, value = op[1], op[2]\n            if key in cache:\n                cache.move_to_end(key)  # update in place, never evicts\n            elif len(cache) == capacity:\n                cache.popitem(last=False)  # drop the least recently used\n            cache[key] = value\n    return results\n",
 "brute": "def solution(capacity, ops):\n    # A dict for values and a plain list for use order (oldest first).\n    # list.remove and pop(0) shift the list, so each op is O(capacity).\n    values = {}\n    order = []\n    results = []\n    for op in ops:\n        key = op[1]\n        if op[0] == \"get\":\n            if key in values:\n                order.remove(key)\n                order.append(key)\n                results.append(values[key])\n            else:\n                results.append(-1)\n        else:\n            if key in values:\n                order.remove(key)\n            elif len(order) == capacity:\n                oldest = order.pop(0)\n                del values[oldest]\n            order.append(key)\n            values[key] = op[2]\n    return results\n",
 "explain": "<p>Walk example 1 with the order written oldest &rarr; newest:</p><ul><li><code>put 1</code>, <code>put 2</code>: <code>[1, 2]</code>.</li><li><code>get 1</code> &rarr; 1, order <code>[2, 1]</code>.</li><li><code>put 3</code>: full and 3 is new, evict the front (2): <code>[1, 3]</code>.</li><li><code>get 2</code> &rarr; -1 (no change).</li><li><code>put 4</code>: evict 1: <code>[3, 4]</code>.</li><li><code>get 1</code> &rarr; -1, <code>get 3</code> &rarr; 3, <code>get 4</code> &rarr; 4.</li></ul><p><code>OrderedDict</code> keeps a hash table for lookup and a doubly linked list for order, so moving a key to the end and dropping the front are O(1). If the interviewer bans it, build that pair yourself: a dict from key to node, and a linked list with dummy head and tail nodes.</p>",
